<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">Great Speeches</h1><p class="ref-lede">28 speeches that moved history, from Pericles to Mandela. Each gives the setting, the famous words with their source, and what followed.</p></header>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The ancient world</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/pericles-funeral-oration"><span class="sp-card-k">Winter 431–430 BCE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Pericles' Funeral Oration</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Our constitution does not copy the laws of neighbouring states; we are rather a pattern to others than imitators ourselves.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Pericles</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/alexander-at-opis"><span class="sp-card-k">324 BCE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Alexander's Speech at Opis</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Philip found you vagabonds and destitute of means, most of you clad in hides, feeding a few sheep up the mountain sides.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Alexander the Great</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/hannibal-before-the-ticinus"><span class="sp-card-k">Autumn 218 BCE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Hannibal to His Army before the Ticinus</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Here, soldiers, you must conquer or die.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Hannibal</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/cicero-against-catiline"><span class="sp-card-k">8 November 63 BCE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Cicero against Catiline</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;How long, Catiline, will you abuse our patience?&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Cicero</span></a></div></section>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The medieval world</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/urban-ii-at-clermont"><span class="sp-card-k">27 November 1095</span><span class="sp-card-t">Pope Urban II at Clermont</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;It is the will of God! It is the will of God!&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Pope Urban II</span></a></div></section>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The early modern world</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/elizabeth-i-at-tilbury"><span class="sp-card-k">August 1588</span><span class="sp-card-t">Elizabeth I at Tilbury</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;I know I have the body but of a weak and feeble woman; but I have the heart and stomach of a king, and of a king of England too.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Elizabeth I</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/charles-i-on-the-scaffold"><span class="sp-card-k">30 January 1649</span><span class="sp-card-t">Charles I on the Scaffold</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;I go from a corruptible to an incorruptible Crown, where no disturbance can be.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Charles I</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/cromwell-dismisses-the-rump"><span class="sp-card-k">20 April 1653</span><span class="sp-card-t">Cromwell Dismisses the Rump Parliament</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;You have sat too long here for any good you have been doing. Depart, I say, and let us have done with you. In the name of God, go!&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Oliver Cromwell</span></a></div></section>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The age of revolutions</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/patrick-henry-liberty-or-death"><span class="sp-card-k">23 March 1775</span><span class="sp-card-t">Give Me Liberty or Give Me Death</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;I know not what course others may take; but as for me, give me liberty or give me death!&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Patrick Henry</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/danton-de-laudace"><span class="sp-card-k">2 September 1792</span><span class="sp-card-t">Danton: Audacity, Always Audacity</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Audacity, more audacity, always audacity, and France will be saved.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Georges Danton</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/napoleon-to-the-army-of-italy"><span class="sp-card-k">March 1796</span><span class="sp-card-t">Napoleon to the Army of Italy</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Soldiers, you are naked and ill fed... I will lead you into the most fertile plains in the world.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Napoleon</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/tecumseh-to-the-osages"><span class="sp-card-k">1811</span><span class="sp-card-t">Tecumseh to the Osages</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Brothers, we all belong to one family; we are all children of the Great Spirit.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Tecumseh</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/bolivar-at-angostura"><span class="sp-card-k">15 February 1819</span><span class="sp-card-t">Bolívar at Angostura</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Unity, unity, unity must be our motto.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Simón Bolívar</span></a></div></section>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The nineteenth century</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/sojourner-truth-aint-i-a-woman"><span class="sp-card-k">May 1851</span><span class="sp-card-t">Sojourner Truth: Ain't I a Woman?</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;And ain't I a woman?&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Sojourner Truth</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/douglass-fourth-of-july"><span class="sp-card-k">5 July 1852</span><span class="sp-card-t">What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;What, to the American slave, is your Fourth of July?&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Frederick Douglass</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/lincoln-gettysburg-address"><span class="sp-card-k">19 November 1863</span><span class="sp-card-t">The Gettysburg Address</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;...that government of the people, by the people, for the people, shall not perish from the earth.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Abraham Lincoln</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/lincoln-second-inaugural"><span class="sp-card-k">4 March 1865</span><span class="sp-card-t">Lincoln's Second Inaugural Address</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;With malice toward none, with charity for all...&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Abraham Lincoln</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/susan-b-anthony-on-suffrage"><span class="sp-card-k">1873</span><span class="sp-card-t">Susan B. Anthony on Women's Right to Vote</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;It was we, the people; not we, the white male citizens; nor yet we, the male citizens; but we, the whole people, who formed the Union.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Susan B. Anthony</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/chief-joseph-surrender"><span class="sp-card-k">5 October 1877</span><span class="sp-card-t">Chief Joseph: I Will Fight No More Forever</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Hear me, my chiefs! I am tired; my heart is sick and sad. From where the sun now stands I will fight no more forever.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Chief Joseph</span></a></div></section>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The twentieth century</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/cixi-reform-edict"><span class="sp-card-k">29 January 1901</span><span class="sp-card-t">The Empress Dowager's Edict of Reform</span><span class="sp-card-q">In substance: The great moral principles never change, but the methods of government must change with the times; China must learn from the strengths of other nations to remedy its own weaknesses.</span><span class="sp-card-by">Empress Dowager Cixi</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/churchill-we-shall-fight"><span class="sp-card-k">4 June 1940</span><span class="sp-card-t">Churchill: We Shall Fight on the Beaches</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;We shall fight on the beaches... we shall never surrender.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Winston Churchill</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/fdr-a-date-which-will-live-in-infamy"><span class="sp-card-k">8 December 1941</span><span class="sp-card-t">Roosevelt: A Date Which Will Live in Infamy</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Yesterday, December 7, 1941 — a date which will live in infamy — the United States of America was suddenly and deliberately attacked by naval and air forces of the Empire of Japan.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Franklin D. Roosevelt</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/gandhi-quit-india"><span class="sp-card-k">8 August 1942</span><span class="sp-card-t">Gandhi: Do or Die</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Here is a mantra, a short one, that I give you... The mantra is: 'Do or Die.'&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Mahatma Gandhi</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/nehru-tryst-with-destiny"><span class="sp-card-k">14 August 1947</span><span class="sp-card-t">Nehru: Tryst with Destiny</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;At the stroke of the midnight hour, when the world sleeps, India will awake to life and freedom.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Jawaharlal Nehru</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/kennedy-ask-not"><span class="sp-card-k">20 January 1961</span><span class="sp-card-t">Kennedy: Ask Not What Your Country Can Do for You</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;And so, my fellow Americans: ask not what your country can do for you — ask what you can do for your country.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">John F. Kennedy</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/eisenhower-farewell-address"><span class="sp-card-k">17 January 1961</span><span class="sp-card-t">Eisenhower's Farewell Address</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;In the councils of government, we must guard against the acquisition of unwarranted influence, whether sought or unsought, by the military-industrial complex.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Dwight D. Eisenhower</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/mlk-i-have-a-dream"><span class="sp-card-k">28 August 1963</span><span class="sp-card-t">Martin Luther King Jr.: I Have a Dream</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;...not be judged by the color of their skin but by the content of their character.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Martin Luther King Jr.</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/mandela-statement-from-the-dock"><span class="sp-card-k">20 April 1964</span><span class="sp-card-t">Mandela's Statement from the Dock</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;It is an ideal for which I am prepared to die.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Nelson Mandela</span></a></div></section>
